Cases for TypeAnalyzerTest. Each case starts with the section sign, the name of
the case set and the name of the scenario. The input and the expectation are
separated by a line of three equals signs.

The cases with names that start with "imprecision" document known imprecisions
of the type analysis. They pin the current behavior so that changes to it are
noticed.

§testExpressions literals:
===
1 => int
1u => uint
1.0 => float
1.0lf => double
true => bool
false => bool
0x1F => int
1.0hf => float16_t
1s => int16_t
1us => uint16_t
1l => int64_t
1ul => uint64_t
(1) => int
((1.0)) => float

§testExpressions references:
int i; const float c = 1.0; struct S { int a; }; int f() { return 1; }
===
i => int
c => float
missing => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
S => <error> ! NOT_A_VARIABLE
f => <error> ! NOT_A_VARIABLE
missing + 1 => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
-missing => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing.x => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing[0] => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing.length() => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing < 1 => bool ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
1 < missing => bool ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing && true => bool ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
true ? missing : 1 => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
true ? 1 : missing => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
true ? missing : missing => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing ? 1 : 2 => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing = 1 => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
i = missing => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
i += missing => int ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
missing++ => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER
i[missing] => <error> ! UNDECLARED_IDENTIFIER INVALID_INDEX

